/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
evaporator10_naoh_triple_effect  --  caustic soda in three effects, kept INSIDE
the window of the heat-of-dilution fit

  WHAT IT IS.  A forward-feed triple-effect NaOH evaporator: live steam heats
  effect 1, the vapour of effect i is the heating chest of effect i+1, the
  vapour of effect 3 leaves to a condenser outside the model.  It is an
  ILLUSTRATION, not a reproduction: the hardware, the feed and the operating
  pressures are those of the forced-circulation triple-effect NaOH problem in
  Geankoplis, Hersel & Lepek, Transport Processes and Separation Process
  Principles, 5th ed., problem 32.5-4 (p. 1529) -- feed 13 608 kg/h of 10 wt %
  NaOH at 37.8 C, steam at 58.6 kPa gauge (160 kPa abs, 113.3 C), 6.76 kPa in
  the last effect, U = 6246 / 3407 / 2271 W/(m2 K), one common area -- but the
  product is 18 wt % (5.5 mol/kg), not the book's 50 wt %.  The reason is the
  window: the calorimetric L_phi fit of the Na-OH Pitzer record is valid to
  `lphiValidityMax 6.0` mol/kg (19.4 wt %), and 50 wt % is 25 mol/kg.  The
  printed answers of that problem (A = 97.3 m2, S = 5284 kg/h) belong to the
  50 wt % target and are NOT comparable to this run; no printed multi-effect
  NaOH example inside the window was found in the books in hand (the only
  printed single-effect caustic example, Example 32.4-3, goes to 50 wt %).

  THE SPECIFICATION (system/outerDict, the structure of evaporator03).  Two
  unknowns, the common area A and the live-steam flow, against two targets:
  the product mass flow 2.1 kg/s (= 1360.8 kg/h NaOH at 18 wt %) and the
  last-effect pressure 6.76 kPa.  Every effect's liquid outlet is inside the
  window, which is what the run's [electrolyte] lines let you check.

  THE RESULT (converged; mass, element and energy closures gated).
    effect   T_boil    P        m          BPE    duty      heat of dilution
              [C]     [kPa]   [mol/kg]    [K]     [kW]     [kW]   [% of duty]
      1       95.6    75.7     3.22       3.57   1880.6     +18.1    +1.0
      2       77.0    35.3     3.96       4.07   1076.1      -5.5    -0.5
      3       43.4     6.76    5.49       5.08   1293.6      -5.2    -0.4
    A = 16.98 m2 per effect; live steam 2981 kg/h; water evaporated
    6048 kg/h; steam economy 2.03 kg vapour per kg steam.
  The BPE is the Pitzer water activity at each effect's outlet molality.  It
  can be held against measured vapour pressures INSIDE the window: Perry's
  Chemical Engineers' Handbook (9th ed.), Table 2-19 (p. 2-83), tabulates the
  partial pressure of water over NaOH solutions; read at each effect's
  pressure and wt % (log-pressure interpolated in 1/T and in concentration;
  about 0.3 K uncertainty) it gives BPE 3.25 / 3.79 / 4.88 K against the
  engine's 3.57 / 4.07 / 5.08 K (+0.3, +0.3, +0.2 K).

  WHERE THE HEAT OF DILUTION ENTERS EACH EFFECT.  The unit's duty is the heat
  the chest gives up condensing, F_chest [h(chest) - h(condensate)], and the
  process side is balanced on the SAME enthalpy surface:
      Q = L h(L, T_out, m_out) + V H_v - F h(F, T_in, m_in).
  The liquor's h carries, for a calorimetrically fitted salt, the term
  n_salt L_phi(m, T).  So the heat of dilution is a REPORTED PART of the duty
  Q, never a term added to it.  The line printed per effect,
      heat of dilution = X kW   (L_phi: m a -> b mol/kg),
  is n_salt [L_phi(m_out, T_out) - L_phi(m_in, T_in)]: the concentration step
  AND the change of L_phi with temperature between the inlet and outlet
  states (the liquor is flashed from the previous effect's temperature to
  the next one's), which is why it is positive in effect 1 (cold feed heated
  to 95 C) and slightly negative downstream.  Beside the latent heat it is
  small here -- about 1 % of each duty -- and that is the lesson of keeping
  inside the window: it is the 6 to 25 mol/kg range of real caustic
  concentration that makes the heat of dilution large (next paragraph).

  WHAT HAPPENS WHEN THE PRODUCT IS PUSHED PAST 6 mol/kg.  The same engine run
  on a single effect (feed 4536 kg/h of 20 wt % at 60 C, 11.7 kPa in the
  vapour space, so pure water boils at 48.9 C -- the setting of Geankoplis
  Example 32.4-3, only the product target changed) returns, announced as
  [electrolyte] ... EXTRAPOLATED, a BPE that stops following the measurement
  and a heat of dilution that climbs from 14 to 464 kW:
    product   m        BPE [K]                                  heat of dilution
    [wt %]  [mol/kg]  engine  Perry T2-19  Geankoplis Fig.32.4-2   engine [kW]
      25      8.3      10.0      9.6           9  (+-2)              14.5
      30     10.7      14.6     14.2          13  (+-2)              43.3
      40     16.7      24.6     27.6          27  (+-2)             169.4
      50     25.0      27.8     47.3          40.6 (printed)        464.4
  Perry's column is computed as above; Geankoplis's is read off the Duhring
  lines of Fig. 32.4-2 (p. 1500) at a water boiling point of 120 F, reading
  uncertainty about 2 K, except the 50 % value, which Example 32.4-3 prints
  (89.5 - 48.9 = 40.6 K, p. 1504).  Up to 30 wt % the engine follows both;
  at 40 % it is 2-3 K low and at 50 % 13-20 K low (a boiling liquid at 76.7 C
  against the book's 89.5 C).  No printed value of the heat of dilution
  alone was found; the closest printed number is the example's duty.  Run on
  the example's own specification (steam at 172.4 kPa, U = 1560 W/(m2 K),
  50 wt % product, designSpec on the area and the steam rate) the engine gives
  2307 kW, 3665 kg/h of steam, A = 38.0 m2, economy 0.74, against the book's
  2002 kW, 3255 kg/h, 49.2 m2, 0.836 (chart readings of the enthalpy-
  concentration chart, Fig. 32.4-3, p. 1503: h = 214 and 505 kJ/kg).  The gap
  mixes two causes that cannot be separated from these numbers -- the BPE
  (the engine's smaller BPE leaves a larger temperature difference, hence the
  smaller area) and the extrapolated L_phi (464 kW of the 2307 kW) -- and
  neither is tuned away.  This is why caustic to 50 wt % is NOT served by this
  record: it needs a property model fitted to that range (DEV.md 4c C377 b).

  THE WATER ROUTE.  The Antoine fit of the catalogue's water record is
  +2.5 % at 373 K and +6 % at 423 K against IAPWS SR1-86, and evaporator08
  runs water at 130 C on it and announces it.  This case carries an ADOPTED
  case-local `constant/components/water.dat` identical to the catalogue's
  except the vapour-pressure block, which is the IAPWS saturation equation
  itself (`model Wagner`; Table 1 check values reproduced to 1.5e-7), so the
  vessel pressures and the Pitzer BPE are on the reference saturation curve
  and the run raises no Antoine-window advisory.  Two limits remain and are
  announced: the liquid Cp of water is the record's constant 75.5 J/(mol K)
  (window 273-373 K; -0.6 % at 373 K, -1 to -2 % near 400 K) and the latent heat of
  the enthalpy surface is about 2 % above the steam table's near 390 K.  The
  fundamental-equation route `pureFluids { water { method IF97; } }` cannot
  be used with a dissolved salt: the electrolyte formulation does not accept
  it, and a mixed liquid phase is refused by the pure-fluid router.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

variables
{
    A          40.0   m2;      // common area, driven by outerDict
}

units
(
    {
        name        effect1;
        type        evaporator;
        inputs      ( feed       liveSteam );
        outputs     ( L1to2  V1  cond1 );
        operation
        {
            area       $A;
            U        6246.0   W/m2/K;
        }
    }
    {
        name        effect2;
        type        evaporator;
        inputs      ( L1to2      V1 );
        outputs     ( L2to3  V2  cond2 );
        operation
        {
            area       $A;
            U        3407.0   W/m2/K;
        }
    }
    {
        name        effect3;
        type        evaporator;
        inputs      ( L2to3      V2 );
        outputs     ( productConc  V3  cond3 );
        operation
        {
            area       $A;
            U        2271.0   W/m2/K;
        }
    }
);
